from core import subjects

def C(id,prompt,options,answer,hint,explanation):
 l=next(l for s in subjects for l in s['lessons'] if l['id']==id)
 q=l['checks'][0];q.update(type='MCQ',prompt=prompt,options=options,answer=answer,hint=hint,explanation=explanation)

C('0-propositions','Which statement is equivalent to P→Q?',['Q→P','¬Q→¬P','¬P→¬Q','P∧Q'],1,'Think about the contrapositive.','The contrapositive preserves the implication: ¬Q→¬P is equivalent to P→Q. Reversing alone gives the converse, which is not equivalent.')
C('0-quantifiers','What is the negation of ∀x∃y R(x,y)?',['∀x∀y ¬R(x,y)','∃x∃y ¬R(x,y)','∃x∀y ¬R(x,y)','∀x∃y ¬R(x,y)'],2,'Swap each quantifier while moving negation inward.','¬∀x∃y R = ∃x¬∃y R = ∃x∀y¬R. One x defeats every candidate y.')
C('0-sets-functions','Which properties define an equivalence relation?',['Reflexive, symmetric, transitive','Reflexive, antisymmetric, transitive','Symmetric and irreflexive','Injective and surjective'],0,'Equivalence classes partition the set.','Reflexivity, symmetry and transitivity define equivalence. Replacing symmetry with antisymmetry defines a partial order instead.')
C('0-orders-lattices','Which condition is required for a poset to be a lattice?',['Every pair is comparable','It has at least one minimal element','Every pair has a meet and a join','Its diagram is a tree'],2,'The requirement concerns pairwise bounds.','Every pair needs a greatest lower bound and a least upper bound. Total comparability is unnecessary; a subset-inclusion lattice can have incomparable elements.')
C('0-algebraic-structures','Nonnegative integers under addition form which structure?',['Group with identity 1','Monoid with identity 0, but not a group','Not closed under addition','Group without an identity'],1,'Ask whether positive elements have inverses inside the set.','Addition is closed and associative with identity 0. Positive integers have no additive inverse among nonnegative integers, so this is not a group.')
C('0-graphs-matching','Which statement distinguishes maximal and maximum matchings?',['They always mean the same thing','Maximal means largest cardinality','Maximum means largest cardinality; maximal means no edge can simply be added','Maximum matchings can be enlarged by adding an edge'],2,'Local inability to add an edge need not prove global optimality.','Maximum optimizes cardinality over all matchings. Maximal only forbids adding an edge to the current set; rearranging chosen edges may yield a larger matching.')
C('0-counting','Choosing 3 people for an unordered committee from 8 people uses which expression?',['8³','8!/(8−3)!','C(8,3)','3!'],2,'Ordering the same three people does not create a different committee.','Use combinations C(8,3). Ordered selections count each committee 3! times.')
C('0-recurrences','What extra factor is needed in an independent solution for a repeated characteristic root λ?',['Only another constant','A factor n multiplying λⁿ','A factor 1/n in every solution','No second independent solution can exist'],1,'Repeated roots require a different-shaped basis solution.','For a double root, independent solutions include λⁿ and nλⁿ. Two constant multiples of λⁿ are not independent.')
C('0-matrices-rank','When is a real linear system Ax=b inconsistent?',['rank(A)=rank([A|b])','rank(A)<number of unknowns','rank(A) differs from rank([A|b])','A is rectangular'],2,'A contradiction in an augmented row adds a rank not present in coefficients.','Consistency requires equal ranks. Lower rank than the unknown count instead gives free variables if the system is consistent.')
C('0-eigen-lu','A repeated eigenvalue implies which claim?',['The matrix is always diagonalizable','The matrix is never diagonalizable','You must check the number of independent eigenvectors','Every vector is necessarily an eigenvector'],2,'Compare 2I with a Jordan-block example.','A repeated eigenvalue may or may not have enough independent eigenvectors. Geometric multiplicity determines the available directions.')
C('0-limits-continuity','Which implication always holds for real functions at a point?',['Continuity implies differentiability','Differentiability implies continuity','A limit guarantees the function is defined','Continuity guarantees zero derivative'],1,'A corner such as |x| separates the two notions.','Differentiability implies continuity. |x| is continuous at zero but its one-sided slopes disagree, so the reverse fails.')
C('0-extrema-mvt','For absolute extrema of a continuous function on [a,b], which candidates must be checked?',['Only points with f′=0','Only endpoints','Endpoints and interior critical points','Every point outside the interval'],2,'A global extremum can occur at a boundary.','Compare endpoint values and values at interior points where derivative is zero or undefined. A zero derivative alone does not classify an extremum.')
C('0-integration','When substituting u=g(x) in a definite integral, what must be kept consistent?',['Only the integrand changes','Differential and limits must match the variable being used','Limits should always remain in x','An arbitrary C must be retained in the final definite result'],1,'Do not use x bounds on a u expression.','Transform the differential and the limits, or substitute back to x before evaluating the original bounds. A definite integral has no arbitrary +C.')
C('0-conditional-bayes','Two disjoint events each have positive probability. Are they independent?',['Always','No','Only because their intersection is zero','Cannot be determined even with disjointness'],1,'Compare zero intersection with the product of positive probabilities.','Their intersection probability is zero while P(A)P(B)>0, violating independence.')
C('0-random-statistics','For continuous X with a density, what is P(X=c) at an individual point?',['The density f(c)','Always 1','Zero','Always the mean'],2,'A single point has zero interval width.','Continuous point probability is zero; interval probability is an integral of density, not a density value.')
C('0-binomial-poisson','Which experiment directly fits a binomial model?',['A fixed number of independent equal-probability success/failure trials','Any count regardless of assumptions','Every waiting time','A continuous value uniformly sampled from an interval'],0,'Identify fixed trials, independence and a common p.','Binomial counts successes under those three assumptions. A rate-based event count may instead use Poisson, and waiting time may be continuous.')
C('0-continuous-distributions','For Normal(μ,σ²), which standardization is correct?',['(X−μ)/σ²','(X−μ)/σ','X/μ','(X+μ)/σ'],1,'Divide by standard deviation, not variance.','Z=(X−μ)/σ centers by the mean and scales by standard deviation.')
C('1-boolean','Which algebraic law explains A+AB=A?',['Absorption','Ordinary numerical subtraction','Associativity alone','Complement of B'],0,'The second term is already contained in the first condition.','Absorption removes AB because whenever AB is true, A is already true; it adds no new accepting assignments.')
C('1-kmap-tabular','Which K-map grouping is allowed?',['Three adjacent cells','Diagonal-only pairs','A power-of-two rectangle that can wrap around edges','A group containing a required zero'],2,'Logical adjacency differs from ordinary diagram distance.','Valid groups are rectangular powers of two in Gray-code adjacency, with wraparound allowed. Required zero cells cannot be used.')
C('1-combinational','Which statement correctly describes a combinational circuit?',['It necessarily stores prior input values','Its output depends on current inputs under the ideal settled model','It requires a clock for every output change','It is the same as a flip-flop'],1,'Separate current-input dependence from stored state.','A combinational function depends on current inputs. Physical propagation delay exists, but there is no designed state memory.')
C('1-sequential','A Moore machine output depends on what?',['Only the current state','Only the next input','Current state and current input in its defining output function','Only the clock frequency'],0,'Compare the Moore and Mealy output definitions.','Moore outputs are functions of state. Mealy outputs can additionally depend directly on current input.')
C('1-representation','When adding two signed integers, which condition signals two’s-complement signed overflow?',['Any carry-out','Equal-sign operands produce an opposite-sign result','Operands have different signs','The result is zero'],1,'Carry and signed overflow are distinct.','At fixed width, adding two positives cannot yield a true negative or two negatives a true positive; an opposite-sign encoded result signals overflow.')
C('2-cache','Which quantity determines cache index bits?',['Total address width alone','Number of sets','Bytes per word alone','Total lines regardless of associativity'],1,'A set-associative cache indexes a set, then compares tags within it.','Index width is log₂(number of sets). Number of sets equals lines divided by ways; total lines works only in direct mapping.')
C('2-memory-io','What is the primary role of DMA?',['Eliminate all memory bus usage','Transfer blocks with reduced per-byte CPU involvement','Replace every interrupt','Execute application instructions'],1,'The CPU configures transfer work but need not move each byte.','DMA transfers between devices and memory with controller support. Setup/completion overhead and bus competition still remain.')
C('2-pipeline','Does a RAW dependency necessarily cause a stall?',['Yes, in every pipeline','No; forwarding may deliver the value on time','No dependency ever stalls','Only control hazards can stall'],1,'Distinguish logical dependency from timing availability.','Forwarding can resolve many RAW dependencies without a stall. A load-use timing gap may still require one in the specified pipeline.')
C('3-c-memory','A C function parameter is written int a[]. Inside the function, what is a’s adjusted parameter type?',['A complete fixed-size array type','Pointer to int','Always a reference type','An integer count'],1,'Array parameter syntax is adjusted.','The parameter is adjusted to int*. sizeof(a) there measures pointer size, not the original caller array storage.')
C('3-recursion','Which determines auxiliary recursive stack use?',['Only total number of calls','Maximum simultaneous call depth and per-frame storage','Only number of return statements','Only the largest returned value'],1,'Frames are released when calls finish.','Stack space follows simultaneously active frames. Many sequential recursive calls may reuse storage, so total calls alone does not give stack size.')
C('3-linear-structures','To implement a singly linked queue efficiently with head/tail pointers, which ends are used?',['Insert at head, delete tail','Insert at tail, delete head','Insert and delete only middle','Search the entire list for every enqueue'],1,'Tail deletion needs finding its predecessor in a singly linked list.','Tail insertion and head deletion take O(1) with maintained pointers. The reverse requires predecessor traversal for dequeue.')
C('3-trees-bst','What does inorder traversal of a BST with distinct keys produce?',['Insertion order','Sorted order','Heap order','Always descending depth'],1,'Left subtree keys are smaller; right subtree keys are larger.','Inorder visits left, root, right, yielding keys in increasing order under the BST invariant.')
C('3-heaps-graphs','Which complexity describes bottom-up binary heap construction?',['Θ(n²)','Θ(n log n)','Θ(n)','Θ(log n)'],2,'Sum work over node heights rather than maximum height times all nodes.','Most nodes have small height; the weighted height sum is linear. Repeated insertion is a different construction method.')
C('4-analysis','A balanced recursion has Θ(log n) depth. Does that prove Θ(log n) running time?',['Yes','No; total work across all branches must be counted','Yes if it has two child calls','Time and stack depth are always equal'],1,'Compare binary search and merge sort.','Depth describes one active path. Branching and combine costs determine total running time; balanced merge sort has log depth but n log n work.')
C('4-greedy-divide','Why does value/weight greedy solve fractional but not necessarily 0/1 knapsack?',['Sorting is unavailable in 0/1 knapsack','Fractional exchange is allowed only in the fractional model','0/1 knapsack never has an optimum','All greedy strategies are invalid'],1,'The counterexample relies on indivisible choices.','Allowing fractional exchange justifies ratio-based choice. Indivisibility can make a combination of lower-ratio items better than greedy selections.')
C('4-dynamic','Why are capacities processed downward in one-dimensional 0/1 knapsack?',['To sort items','To avoid using the same item again in its iteration','To increase memory use','Because all DP loops must descend'],1,'An updated lower state can otherwise be reused by a later larger state.','Descending order preserves prior-item values for dp[w−weight]. Upward order can count the current item repeatedly, changing the problem.')
C('5-automata','An NFA accepts a string when which condition holds?',['Every possible path accepts','At least one complete path ends in an accepting state','It visits an accepting state before input ends','Its state count exceeds input length'],1,'Acceptance is existential over full-input paths.','At least one path must consume the entire input and reach an accepting state. Other failing paths do not prevent acceptance.')
C('5-regular-pumping','In a nonregularity pumping proof, who controls a permitted decomposition xyz?',['The proof may choose just its favourite split','The opponent; the proof must handle every valid split','Nobody because the split is unique','The alphabet alone'],1,'Track the quantifier order.','After p and your selected word, you must show every allowed split fails for some pumping exponent. One failed chosen split is insufficient.')
C('5-cfg-pda','Which closure claim is valid?',['CFLs are closed under arbitrary intersection','CFLs are closed under intersection with regular languages','CFLs are closed under every complement','All CFLs are regular'],1,'A regular restriction preserves context-free recognition.','A PDA can be combined with finite-state regular constraints. Intersection of two arbitrary CFLs need not be context free.')
C('5-decidability','What distinguishes a decider from a recognizer?',['A decider has no tape','A decider halts on every input','A recognizer cannot accept anything','A decider needs infinitely many states'],1,'Consider negative instances.','A decider halts and answers on every input; a recognizer may loop on nonmembers while still eventually accepting every member.')
C('6-parsing','Which set can contain ε?',['FOLLOW only','FIRST, when the expression is nullable','Neither can ever contain it','Every FOLLOW set must contain it'],1,'FOLLOW tracks terminals that can occur after a nonterminal.','FIRST may include ε for a nullable expression. FOLLOW includes possible following terminals and sometimes the end marker, not ε.')
C('6-ir-dataflow','Which join is used for live-variable OUT sets at multiple successors?',['Intersection','Union','Arithmetic multiplication','Always the empty set'],1,'A variable is live if some future path needs it.','Liveness is a may-property; union includes variables needed on any successor continuation.')
C('7-deadlock','Which statement about an unsafe resource state is correct?',['It is necessarily already deadlocked','It lacks a guaranteed safe completion sequence but may not be deadlocked yet','It always has zero available resources','It violates every Coffman condition'],1,'Avoidance reasons about possible future demands.','Unsafe means no safe sequence under declared maximum demands. Current requests may still permit progress, so actual deadlock is not established.')
C('7-virtual-files','A TLB miss implies which conclusion?',['The page is definitely on disk','The translation is not currently found in the TLB; the page may still be resident','The address has no offset','The process must terminate'],1,'TLB entries cache translations.','A page-table lookup may find a valid resident frame. A TLB miss alone does not mean a page fault.')
C('8-queries','Which SQL clause filters groups after aggregation?',['WHERE','HAVING','FROM','DISTINCT'],1,'Group predicates require already formed groups.','HAVING filters groups; WHERE filters source rows before aggregation.')
C('8-normalization','What is the BCNF rule for a nontrivial functional dependency X→Y?',['X must be a superkey','Y must be the primary key','X must be a single attribute','Every attribute must be prime'],0,'BCNF removes the prime-RHS exception from 3NF.','Every nontrivial determinant must be a superkey. A prime RHS can permit 3NF but does not by itself satisfy BCNF.')
C('8-transactions','Which pair creates a conflict between distinct transactions on the same item?',['Read followed by read','A pair containing at least one write','Every pair even on different items','Only writes in the same transaction'],1,'Conflict requires different transactions, same item and a write.','Read/write, write/read and write/write pairs conflict. Read/read does not.')
C('9-link-layer','Which statement about modern full-duplex switched Ethernet is correct?',['It uses CSMA/CD on every link','It does not use collision detection in the classic shared-medium sense','It has no framing','It routes by longest IP prefix at every basic switch'],1,'Separate full-duplex links from a shared half-duplex collision domain.','Full-duplex switched Ethernet does not use CSMA/CD; classic half-duplex shared-medium Ethernet does.')
C('9-tcp-web','TCP flow control primarily protects which resource?',['Receiver buffering capacity','Only router queues','DNS naming hierarchy','HTTP cache freshness'],0,'Compare receive window with congestion window.','The receiver advertises rwnd to avoid overrunning its capacity. Congestion control addresses network load through cwnd.')
C('9-sockets','Does one TCP send call always correspond to exactly one recv call?',['Yes','No; TCP preserves bytes, not application message boundaries','Yes if the message is short','Only when DNS is cached'],1,'TCP can split or combine delivered byte portions.','Applications need framing and partial-I/O handling because send and recv call boundaries are not preserved as messages.')
